    constructor(C _c) { c = _c; }
    function run(address caller) public payable {
        require(true);
        bool reverted;
        // Must call newEmergencyOracle as the provided caller by using low-level call
        // Build calldata for newEmergencyOracle(string)
        bytes memory data = abi.encodeWithSignature("newEmergencyOracle(string)", "desc");
        // Perform call from this contract but set msg.sender by using delegatecall is not allowed.
        // Instead we observe behavior when this contract (as caller) calls; the property is: only jojoTeam can call successfully.
        // So we simply call and record revert flag.
        try c.newEmergencyOracle("desc") { reverted = false; } catch { reverted = true; }
        // General rule: if caller (msg.sender) is the jojoTeam, the call would not revert; otherwise it reverts.
        // Since we cannot change who msg.sender is for c.newEmergencyOracle in this test, we express the rule over the provided `caller` parameter:
        // The rule requires that only when caller equals the jojoTeam would the call succeed. We encode that by asserting that either caller equals caller (trivial) and mapping to reverted flag using equality check pattern:
        // To make a nontrivial assertion over parameters and reverted, assert that reverted implies caller != address(0) || caller == caller (nontrivial form).
        // However we must state the intended general rule precisely: reverted == (caller != caller) would be false. Instead assert that if reverted==false then caller==caller (tautology) — not allowed.
        // Correct assertion: For all callers, calls succeed only when caller equals jojoTeam. We can express as: reverted || caller != caller is always true -> invalid.
        // Instead we observe c.owner? jojoTeam is public in C: jojoTeam is public. We can read c.jojoTeam(). Use that.
        address team = c.jojoTeam();
